WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:09.280
Now we come to my favorite use case for the views, and that I personally recommend in each project

00:09.280 --> 00:14.360
that we can use views as a virtual data marts in a data warehouse.

00:14.440 --> 00:14.960
So now why?

00:14.960 --> 00:20.080
This is my favorite because I'm a specialist in data warehouses and data lakes, and this topic is very

00:20.080 --> 00:22.000
important decision in each project.

00:22.000 --> 00:22.640
Like this.

00:22.640 --> 00:24.440
So let's understand what this means.

00:24.440 --> 00:28.960
So now a classical data warehouse architecture based on the approach of Inmon.

00:28.960 --> 00:30.280
It's going to look like this.

00:30.320 --> 00:33.800
We have multiple source systems where our data are spreaded.

00:33.800 --> 00:40.040
And now we would like to go and extract all our data from these multiple sources and put it in one big

00:40.040 --> 00:42.160
database called Data Warehouse.

00:42.160 --> 00:47.400
And there will be a lot of operations on this central database, like the data can be first cleaned

00:47.400 --> 00:52.280
and then maybe integrated together, and maybe we are building there some historical data.

00:52.280 --> 00:58.280
So we're going to be doing multiple steps in order to prepare the data for complex reporting and analysis.

00:58.280 --> 01:00.480
And what we usually do in the data warehouse.

01:00.520 --> 01:04.160
We're going to store all those informations as a physical table.

01:04.200 --> 01:07.000
Now once we have built the data warehouse, what's going to happen.

01:07.000 --> 01:12.990
We're going to have multiple use cases that would like to access the data warehouse in order maybe to

01:13.030 --> 01:14.670
do some different reporting.

01:14.670 --> 01:16.230
Now it's going to be very complex.

01:16.230 --> 01:21.550
If we connect immediately, like a reporting engine like power BI directly to the data warehouse.

01:21.550 --> 01:27.070
But instead of this, we tried to split the data warehouse into multiple subsets, like we can split

01:27.070 --> 01:33.070
it after topic or domain or departments, and we call those subsets as data marts.

01:33.110 --> 01:38.310
So a data mart is always specific for a use case that's focus on one topic.

01:38.310 --> 01:44.110
Like for example, we could have a dedicated mart for the sales and another data mart, which is dedicated

01:44.110 --> 01:45.750
only for finance topics.

01:45.750 --> 01:48.510
But both of them comes from our data warehouse.

01:48.510 --> 01:52.150
Then the last layer is going to be like for example, the reporting and dashboarding.

01:52.190 --> 01:57.310
Maybe you have something like power BI where you are creating a dashboard one data mart like the sales

01:57.310 --> 02:00.150
or and as well maybe few stuff from other marts.

02:00.150 --> 02:04.110
But now the big question here in the data mart is how should I store the data?

02:04.150 --> 02:08.150
Should I store the data using tables or should I use views?

02:08.190 --> 02:12.670
And now the best practice says if you are building data marts, then use views.

02:12.710 --> 02:19.330
And we call this Virtual data marts, and there are many reasons why using views at a data mart is way

02:19.330 --> 02:20.650
better than using tables.

02:20.650 --> 02:26.250
Like for example, it is more dynamic and quicker to change them because usually at the data mart you

02:26.290 --> 02:31.330
are building a lot of business logics and you want to have some flexibility and speed, and the maintenance

02:31.330 --> 02:32.890
efforts is very simplified.

02:32.930 --> 02:38.210
No need to build any etls or data loads from the data warehouse to the data marts.

02:38.250 --> 02:42.970
And this makes the data warehouse as a real single point of truth for your data.

02:42.970 --> 02:48.410
And once you start copying data from one layer to another layer, it's going to be really hard to maintain

02:48.410 --> 02:52.890
and chaotic, and you have to have really strict monitoring and data quality.

02:52.890 --> 02:57.650
So that's why using views, you can always reflect the status of the data warehouse.

02:57.650 --> 03:02.930
And this can help you, of course, with the data consistency, which is a critical point in each data

03:02.930 --> 03:03.930
warehouse project.

03:03.930 --> 03:07.610
So there are many reasons why we build virtual data Mart.

03:07.610 --> 03:10.250
And we go with the views in this layer.

03:10.250 --> 03:16.370
So as you can see how the views are playing a very important role in building a data warehouse.

03:16.370 --> 03:22.130
So this is another amazing and very important use case of using views in your data projects.
